00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.270
understanding people wanna hear that story,

259
00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:03.884
but not wanting to dwell on the past,

260
00:11:03.884 --> 00:11:07.610
and really being more focused on

261
00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.110
all the great things that you're doing now

262
00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:10.800
that you're doing so well.

263
00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:11.633
And I was like, "Cool."

264
00:11:11.633 --> 00:11:15.020
And after our run and Alexi texted me

265
00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.045
'cause she had additional thoughts.

266
00:11:17.045 --> 00:11:18.314
Of course she always has.

267
00:11:18.314 --> 00:11:19.780
Right of course.

268
00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:21.540
And she said, "Mary is doing great,

269
00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.270
but there's always grieving to do.

270
00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:25.240
And I think she's doing a phenomenal job

271
00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.320
of observing and allowing that grief

272
00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.770
while also growing up and crafting a life she wants

273
00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.560
before her own eyes, so it's both.

274
00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:36.660
She's great, and she knows she has all that inside."

275
00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.500
That's amazing.

276
00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.670
Alexi just has a way with words that I feel

277
00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:42.350
the number of times after we've had

278
00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:45.010
a 90 minute conversation over the phone,

279
00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:46.940
and we say our goodbyes,

280
00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:49.460
five minutes later, she ends up sending a paragraph

281
00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:51.600
that just literally makes me tear up

282
00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.270
about how much our friendship means to her,

283
00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.530
or just some inspirational thing based on

284
00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.370
a different topic that we had talked about

285
00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.810
over the course of the conversation.

286
00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:05.560
And I always feel my reply is probably not as eloquent.

287
00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:08.080
But she knows.
Well, she always said

288
00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.400
she was gonna go get her PhD in poetry,

289
00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:11.233
and her book,

290
00:12:11.233 --> 00:12:13.160
I mean, not everybody can write a book like that,

291
00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:14.320
it's quite something.

292
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.410
It was beautiful.

293
00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:18.950
I mean, I was one of the first people who got to read it,

294
00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.920
and I think I finished it in two days,

295
00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.530
and I just the whole time would be texting her stuff.

296
00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:25.780
And some of the stories,

297
00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.780
even though we are very good friends, I didn't know,

298
00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:33.050
and a lot of it was on that personal level for her

299
00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.080
where many people who were very close to her

300
00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.053
didn't have that full picture.

301
00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.890
And being able to

302
00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.160
get to be one of the first people to see that,

303
00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:45.150
and experience that, and then talk to her about it

304
00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:48.080
felt very special.

305
00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.630
And conversely, she was not aware

306
00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:53.650
of all the things that you were going through at the time.

307
00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:57.420
It's that thing where you're in that pain,

308
00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:00.800
and you silo yourself off even from your closest friend

309
00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:02.540
'cause you guys were close back then,

310
00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.140
but neither of you were really communicating with each other

311
00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.960
on that deeper level about what you were enduring privately.

312
00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:10.980
Yeah, absolutely.

313
00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:12.010
And I think in many ways

314
00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:15.680
the reason we are both very vocal now about mental health,

315
00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:19.530
depression, and the need to talk about it,

316
00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.620
and the need to seek help, and lean on other people

317
00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.940
is because we reflect on our own journeys,

318
00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:27.580
which have shockingly overlapped,

319
00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:29.187
even on a year to year it's like,

320
00:13:29.187 --> 00:13:31.967
"Wow, 2016, what happened in that year?"

321
00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.480
And as you kind of look,

322
00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.090
I think as we look back, there's this ability to see

323
00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.300
maybe if we could've, should've, would've

324
00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.470
and leaned on each other,

325
00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.010
things would have gone a little differently.

326
00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:49.030
And that's not to say

327
00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.080
you can talk yourself out of depression,

328
00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:51.913
that's not to say

329
00:13:51.913 --> 00:13:55.230
you can talk yourself out of a mental health struggle,

330
00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:56.450
but at least being able to know

331
00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:58.000
somebody else is going through it,

332
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.030
and maybe working together to find the resources

333
00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:05.530
to get us help, could have saved us both sooner.

334
00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:07.720
And knowing that makes me always think

335
00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.690
how can I help somebody else take that step earlier?

336
00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:11.523
Yeah.

337
00:14:11.523 --> 00:14:14.130
Well, I think Alexi writing her book

338
00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.830
and you sharing your story

339
00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:19.860
in the way that you have been doing is the path forward

340
00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.440
because that gives other people the comfort,

341
00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.440
and the empowerment required to share something that

342
00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:32.590
privately they feel maybe they can't, right?

343
00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:35.250
I know you're a big Brené Brown fan.

344
00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.000
Yes.
And she's all about that

345
00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:40.640
can you summon the courage to be vulnerable on that level?

346
00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.740
Yeah, and I think for me it's always come back to,

347
00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:48.740
and I just say this in that as honest way as I can that

348
00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:50.977
for me, it always was like,

349
00:14:50.977 --> 00:14:53.390
"Yes, this is gonna help me in my own healing journey,

350
00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:54.630
completely.

351
00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:58.100
If I share, then it's kind of like putting,"

352
00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:01.570
sometimes putting words out there can create truth

353
00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.683
in a way that thoughts can't quite do and,

354
00:15:04.683 --> 00:15:06.620
it's the same reason why during a workout

355
00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.150
sometimes you have to verbalize, "I got this,"

356
00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:11.700
just feel that extra energy.

357
00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.360
And so that kind of maybe selfish reason

358
00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:15.533
is part of the reason I shared my story,

359
00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:18.510
but so much more so it was,

360
00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:22.810
it's scary to realize that so many mental health injuries,

361
00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.203
as Alexi would call them,

362
00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:28.220
are something that even if you have it,

363
00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:31.170
you can't recognize in the same way.

364
00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:33.300
And I think physical injuries, especially as runners,

365
00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.270
we can relate to this,

366
00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.440
who hasn't run through an injury

367
00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:39.840
for two weeks longer than they should have

368
00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.740
'cause they convinced themselves it was something else,

369
00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.000
or something they could work through,

370
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.473
or et cetera, et cetera,

371
00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.470
but issues that relate to mental health,

372
00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:51.550
I feel like since you can't physically see it,

373
00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:54.350
since you can't get that MRI diagnosis,

374
00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:57.430
and even since it's harder to find medical professionals

375
00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.770
who are always going to really know

376
00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.883
how to help you as an individual,

377
00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:05.550
I think it's just harder

378
00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.360
to sometimes recognize what you're actually going through.

379
00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:11.100
And so, the big reason that I came forward,

380
00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.620
and I know the big reason that Alexi is sharing her story,

381
00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:17.800
is because if it can help one other person

382
00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.720
put a name to what their experience is,

383
00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:25.313
then the ability for them to seek help is so much higher.

384
00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:27.740
And I know for me personally

385
00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.070
I always kind of explain to people that

386
00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:35.070
I knew in 2016 that I had forms of disordered eating,

387
00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.000
but I didn't know why,

388
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.130
I didn't know what that kind of deeper,

389
00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.860
darker depression was underneath

390
00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.990
that was predisposing me to have these tendencies.

391
00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:52.580
And it wasn't until I was able to actually realize that

392
00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.000
emotional abuse by the hands of a coach

393
00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:59.240
was what was always holding me back from truly

394
00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.910
both getting the help that I needed,

395
00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:04.740
but also knowing how to get the help that I needed.

396
00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:08.620
It just made all those years before not pointless,

397
00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:11.420
but not as successful as they could have been,

398
00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.677
fruitful, and positive, and all those great things.

399
00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.980
And instead it wasn't until 2019,

400
00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.970
really around the time that I was sharing in the story,

401
00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:26.743
that I was suddenly able to really get help.

402
00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.340
Well, you can point,

403
00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:34.300
you can kind of identify a couple primary reasons for that.

404
00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:36.250
I mean, first of all there was,

405
00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:40.200
of course there are women who have spoken publicly

406
00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.770
about RED-S, and disordered eating in sport,

407
00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:43.910
and all that all the like,

408
00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:46.160
but perhaps not on the scale

409
00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.420
with which your story seemed to connect.

410
00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:51.980
I mean, it so crazy viral when the video was published.

411
00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:54.920
And I wanna talk about that, but at the same time,

412
00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:59.260
from a systemic point of view, you're on this team that

413
00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.380
on paper look like everything was dialed in,

414
00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:05.620
and all the support that any professional athlete

415
00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.860
at the elite level, whatever need to succeed,

416
00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:10.877
including quote unquote,

417
00:18:10.877 --> 00:18:13.020
"Performance psychologists and nutritionists,"

418
00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.450
that turned out weren't that at all, right?

419
00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:20.450
So you can't really be too hard on yourself

420
00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:24.490
because being as young as you were, and not knowing,

421
00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:27.890
not being able to point two examples out in the world

422
00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.390
that would help you kind of map out where you were at,

423
00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:32.760
and not having the internal support

424
00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.330
and the kind of situation, the dynamic that was at play

425
00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.850
at the Oregon project at the time,

426
00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:42.610
you were kind of left to fend for yourself with all of this.

427
00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:45.420
Yeah, and I think now, today as I sit here,

428
00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:47.080
I totally recognize that.

429
00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:48.550
And I think the tough thing is,

430
00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:51.200
and I think why in so many ways

431
00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:54.173
the video went more viral than I had expected,

432
00:18:55.070 --> 00:19:00.010
is because logically that's true,

433
00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:03.890
but emotionally, it can take years for somebody to recognize

434
00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:05.350
that they're not at fault.

435
00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:08.020
And I think a lot of that is just societally

436
00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.770
we have almost created this

437
00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.140
horrible and toxic perspective that

438
00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.087
all mental health is somehow a personal feeling,

439
00:19:16.087 --> 00:19:19.230
and it kind of goes back to that Calvinistic

440
00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:23.910
where you are is because of who you are in a way,

441
00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.893
and I hate that thinking and it's horrible,

442
00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.170
but I think that really kind of filters into

443
00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:31.350
our society at large.

444
00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:35.590
And as a result what's really difficult

445
00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:36.930
for I think a lot of young people

446
00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:40.090
is that when you're in these really toxic,

447
00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.253
and horrible, and abusive situations,

448
00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:45.430
as you're breaking down,

449
00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:46.907
you start to see this quote unquote

450
00:19:46.907 --> 00:19:48.610
"Truth in what they're saying"

451
00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:49.443
because you are like,

452
00:19:49.443 --> 00:19:50.700
"Yeah, I am super emotional.

453
00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:52.550
I'm crying every single day.

454
00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:55.820
Maybe I am the problem 'cause I'm the one crying,"

455
00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.830
or, "Yes, I'm the one that they're telling is overweight.

456
00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:03.427
So, that must be something I'm doing wrong."

457
00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:06.540
And I think it's so easy to get therefore

458
00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:10.270
into this really toxic self blame perspective,

459
00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:12.950
and that's not to say there are things I look back

460
00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.247
and I'm like,

461
00:20:14.247 --> 00:20:17.740
"Oh, maybe, had I just know to be more confident,

462
00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:22.740
or do this," and you always kick yourself in some ways,

463
00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:27.400
but I think I've made peace with my past in a way that

464
00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:30.590
being able to share publicly,

465
00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:33.490
and almost develop this relationship

466
00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.630
with thousands and millions of people

467
00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:39.240
who were able to watch that

468
00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:42.400
and kind of see themselves in my story

469
00:20:43.424 --> 00:20:45.380
is both really tragic,

470
00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:48.080
but also beautiful in that healing process

471
00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.700
of knowing you're not alone.

472
00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:50.720
Right.

473
00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.510
We should probably sort of

474
00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:54.620
establish the terrain that we're talking about

475
00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.750
for people that are watching or listening

476
00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.840
who aren't familiar with the trajectory of your story,

477
00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:02.300
and how it became a viral moment.

478
00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.110
So why don't we just spend a few minutes

479
00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:05.710
talking about your background?

480
00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:09.030
So you grow up outside of New York City, Bronxville.

481
00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:11.120
You run in high school, you're crushing it,

