00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:57.407
now I have to say quote unquote, "Foreign psychologist,"

521
00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.287
there was more positivity and everything was kind of like,

522
00:23:02.287 --> 00:23:03.607
"Oh, this is fun."

523
00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:09.730
And then I ended up after my indoor season senior year

524
00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:12.710
developing a stress reaction in my shin.

525
00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:16.820
And that was kind of a moment that things changed.

526
00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.400
And I think truthfully,

527
00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:21.720
and just at least how I kind of look back on it,

528
00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:24.160
I think having a little bit of time off

529
00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:26.810
my body started develop a little bit, right?

530
00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:27.643
Nothing,

531
00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.320
I didn't all of a sudden

532
00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:32.670
gain 10 pounds or anything like that,

533
00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:36.220
it wasn't something you could even necessarily visually see,

534
00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:38.360
but physically I could tell

535
00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:40.770
there was some sort of changes starting to happen

536
00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:45.350
'cause I'm all of a sudden at the end of that high school,

537
00:23:45.350 --> 00:23:46.520
going into college years,

538
00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:49.560
this is when a lot of women start to develop.

539
00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:50.450
And so, all of a sudden

540
00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:52.770
weight started to become a conversation

541
00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:56.253
in a way that in the past it hadn't really been,

542
00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:59.487
and it would kind of start off small of being like,

543
00:23:59.487 --> 00:24:01.500
"Oh, you're cross-training,

544
00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:03.720
you have to be more conscientious of your weight,"

545
00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:08.120
and it just kept building and building and building.

546
00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:11.740
So by the time I was kind of in full swing

547
00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:14.090
of the outdoor season,

548
00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:16.850
I ended up coming in second at the US championships,

549
00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:21.850
ran a 4:06, and was feeling really good and really positive.

550
00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:24.600
But I kept being told that

551
00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:26.710
I couldn't go to Europe that year to race

552
00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.183
because I wasn't fit enough.

553
00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:29.960
From Alberto.

554
00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:31.000
Yes.

555
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:32.810
And it was this kind of like, "You're not fit,

556
00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:34.730
you're not fit, you're not fit."

557
00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:35.653
And I didn't,

558
00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:37.343
I kind of was like,

559
00:24:37.343 --> 00:24:40.020
"I kept sinking at the US championship, I think I'm unfit."

560
00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:42.830
There's one person maybe a little bit more fit than me?

561
00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:43.680
Literally, yes.

562
00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:47.000
And so, I just didn't really get it,

563
00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:50.220
and I ended up racing at the World Junior Championships.

564
00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:51.760
And the day before the race

565
00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:54.763
I was told to do two 100 meters all out.

566
00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.680
And I was told to break 13 seconds for the 100.

567
00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.290
And I ended up running 12:5 for both of them.

568
00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:02.860
So I didn't think anything of it,

569
00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:04.020
besides the fact that it's kind of weird

570
00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.870
to do all out sprints the day before a race,

571
00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:10.870
but after the race, Alberto, kind of that week, I was like,

572
00:25:11.327 --> 00:25:14.333
"Hey, World Junior Champion, I'm second at USA,

573
00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:16.990
let's dive in, let's go to Europe,

574
00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:18.590
let's do what we did last year."

575
00:25:19.765 --> 00:25:22.110
And that was when it finally became clear that

576
00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:23.883
this fitness was about weight.

577
00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:27.260
And it was because he told me that

578
00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:30.510
the reason he had me do those 100s the day before

579
00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:34.120
is that he wanted to have the talk the day before the race

580
00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:36.880
that I was too heavy,

581
00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:39.480
and he wanted me to do those 100s

582
00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.380
'cause he didn't think I would be physically capable

583
00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:45.123
of breaking 13 'cause he thought I was too big to do it.

584
00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:48.340
But then you go 12:5,

585
00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.650
and he didn't eat his lunch after that.

586
00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:53.520
No, he still told me after the race that

587
00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:56.100
even though I did that,

588
00:25:56.100 --> 00:26:00.353
it's clear I'm not skinny enough to travel.

589
00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:04.130
So of course I'm,

590
00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:08.110
just graduated high school, literally moved out to Oregon

591
00:26:08.110 --> 00:26:11.520
right after winning world juniors.

592
00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.640
And rather than traveling to Europe,

593
00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:17.260
we decide I'm gonna stay in Portland over the summer,

594
00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:20.713
and I'm going to get fit, aka lose weight.

595
00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.270
And there was kind of this idea that

596
00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:27.250
if I lost one pound per week,

597
00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:31.370
I could get down to 113 pounds to race at Fifth Ave,

598
00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:33.220
and therefore I might be competitive.

599
00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:37.820
And granted again, I came in second at USA, so I don't,

600
00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:40.963
in retrospect, I should have just thought what the heck,

601
00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:44.740
but this is hypothetically the best coach in the world.

602
00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:46.180
Sure.

603
00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:48.970
I mean, we're gonna dive deeper into the whole weight thing,

604
00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:52.140
but on some level, when you tell that story,

605
00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:55.920
it almost feels like a way in which

606
00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:58.280
he can exert greater control.

607
00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:01.143
Is it a control issue as much as it is a weight issue

608
00:27:01.143 --> 00:27:04.230
because as long as he can Lord that over you,

609
00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:06.710
then he can kind of be the puppeteer.

610
00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:08.490
Yeah, absolutely.

611
00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:10.500
And I think we recognize that now,

612
00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:14.120
in retrospect with other kind of behaviors that happened.

613
00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:17.660
Like when I was in high school he communicated,

614
00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:21.560
and all the team communicated a lot with my parents, right?

615
00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:24.177
To be like after a good workout, there would be this like,

616
00:27:24.177 --> 00:27:26.100
"Wow, Mary did awesome.

617
00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:27.537
So cool. Congrats."

618
00:27:28.421 --> 00:27:32.590
And it felt this really kind of like family, team dynamic,

619
00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:35.413
and everything's just so positive.

620
00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:38.653
As soon as I went out to Portland,

621
00:27:39.580 --> 00:27:42.170
all communication with my parents stopped.

622
00:27:42.170 --> 00:27:47.170
And what happened was that after world juniors,

623
00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:50.920
as the dedicated athlete that I was who,

624
00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:52.500
yes again, in retrospect

625
00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.730
I was racing at this really high level,

626
00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:57.610
but to me I'd been top 10 in the world,

627
00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:00.470
I'm trying to be a world champion,

628
00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:02.350
that's kind of like my end game.

629
00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:04.890
So when somebody is telling me, "You are not fit enough,

630
00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.219
'Well, yeah, I was second at USA's,

631
00:28:07.219 --> 00:28:09.300
maybe that's top 20 in the world,

632
00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.290
and I'm constantly pushing.'"

633
00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:14.250
So I did what I was told

634
00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:18.610
and I lost probably six to seven pounds

635
00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:20.893
'cause I think I was about 120 at the time,

636
00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:24.243
scaled down to 113.

637
00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:28.739
And that was when disordered eating started.

638
00:28:28.739 --> 00:28:31.370
And how was your performance that year?

639
00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.250
Oh, it ended up pretty cool.

640
00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:35.700
I mean, now I look back I'm like, "It wasn't that bad,"

641
00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:38.150
but it was horrible compared to the years before.

642
00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:43.253
But what happened was after Fifth Ave,

643
00:28:43.253 --> 00:28:45.783
I didn't race that well, I came in eighth,

644
00:28:46.973 --> 00:28:48.403
and I didn't look good.

645
00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:51.730
My parents saw me 'cause we were back in New York,

646
00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:56.480
and truthfully they had felt a little distanced from me

647
00:28:57.320 --> 00:28:58.420
and from the program,

648
00:28:58.420 --> 00:29:00.650
and it was kind of this weird last two months

649
00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:03.383
of being a little bit ghosted.

650
00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:07.547
And my mom saw me and was just like,

651
00:29:07.547 --> 00:29:09.200
"You don't look good.

652
00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:11.460
You look pale, dark circles under your eyes.

653
00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.490
What's kind of going on?"'

654
00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:18.750
And I admitted that I've been really working to lose weight.

655
00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:22.370
I've been told I was too heavy to travel.

656
00:29:22.370 --> 00:29:25.840
And my parents naturally got really upset about that

657
00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.180
because they're like, "What are you even talking about?

658
00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:29.520
You raced great.

659
00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:30.824
Why is that a conversation?"

660
00:29:30.824 --> 00:29:34.163
And a phone call was had with the,

661
00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.350
I'll call him high-performance coach on the team,

662
00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:39.730
and the coach-
Right this person

663
00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.730
sort of held himself to be a psychologist,

664
00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:45.100
but it turns out he was not indeed a psychologist.

665
00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:48.990
Yes, exactly, and I thought he was a medical professional

666
00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:52.653
and therefore treated our conversations as such.

667
00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:58.750
But I afterwards got in big trouble for telling my parents

668
00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:06.090
about my struggles with weight and everything.

669
00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:07.810
And I got this big lecture,

670
00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:11.860
which continued throughout that year,

671
00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:15.210
where if I ever kind of like maybe told my parents something

672
00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:16.450
that I was uncomfortable with,

673
00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:20.690
or whether it was about taking birth control,

674
00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:25.690
or an inhaler, or a thyroid medication, or whatever it was,

675
00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:29.310
if I told them that then I would get yelled at

676
00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.100
because I had to be an adult, I had to be a professional,

677
00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:36.740
you can't go crying to your parents, all this sort of stuff.

678
00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:40.950
And as an 18 year old who's at college, most freshmen.

679
00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:41.810
Yeah, that's great.

680
00:30:41.810 --> 00:30:43.380
I mean, you're 18 years old,

681
00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:45.307
that's a crazy control thing, right?

682
00:30:45.307 --> 00:30:46.380
"You're 18 years old,

683
00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.000
how dare you tell your parents

684
00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.570
what's going on in your life?"

685
00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:53.360
And what's interesting about that is the fact that

686
00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:58.305
Salazar's defense or rebuttal in the wake of

687
00:30:58.305 --> 00:31:00.350
you coming out and sharing your story

688
00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:02.840
is that he's claiming that

689
00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:05.330
he was in constant communion with your parents

690
00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:06.880
and talking to them about weight,

691
00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:08.780
your dad's a doctor, right?

692
00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:10.433
So is that just not true?
No.

693
00:31:10.433 --> 00:31:12.570
I mean, it sounds like from your point of view

694
00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:13.820
that it didn't happen at all.

695
00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:16.480
So, when I was in high school again, they talked a lot.

696
00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:19.430
There was a lot of this back and forth,

697
00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:22.670
emailing, calling, super positive, right?

698
00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.730
I move out to Portland, all of that ends.

699
00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.080
And the only time my parents are hearing anything

700
00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:29.650
is if it's coming from me.

701
00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.620
I then get yelled at for telling them anything.

702
00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:34.710
And then if they call and have this conversation,

703
00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:35.543
it's kind of like,

704
00:31:35.543 --> 00:31:38.007
"I'm a professional coach. I know what I'm doing."

705
00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:41.380
And I think one thing about this

706
00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:45.640
the whole situation as well as my dad is a doctor,

707
00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:48.500
but if you meet any good doctor, right?

708
00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.150
If something's not their area of expertise,

709
00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:51.983
they're always gonna be like,

710
00:31:51.983 --> 00:31:55.830
"No, go to somebody who is an OB-GYN, is an orthopedist,

711
00:31:55.830 --> 00:31:57.047
et cetera, et cetera."

712
00:31:58.130 --> 00:32:02.377
And I think there kind of was this feeling of like,

713
00:32:02.377 --> 00:32:03.930
"This is a Nike.

714
00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:07.450
This is a team that has a multi-million dollar budget.

715
00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:10.220
Is paying people high amounts of money.

716
00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:14.750
Is kind of presenting themselves as this

717
00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:17.240
incredibly professional organization,

718
00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:19.187
one of a kind situation."

719
00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:21.560
And if they're being told that

720
00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:23.900
I'm working with somebody for my mental health,

721
00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:27.430
and and nutrition, and all that sort of stuff,

722
00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:29.510
they're gonna assume that true.

723
00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:30.860
Right, and that it's gonna be

724
00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:32.437
somebody who's really good, right?

725
00:32:32.437 --> 00:32:33.270
Exactly, yeah.

726
00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:37.490
But this guy, this sports psychologist, Darren Treasure,

727
00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:38.403
that was his name.

728
00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:45.210
I read, I think this was in Chris Chavez's side piece,

729
00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.820
that anything that any of the athletes shared with that guy

730
00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.460
just ended up back with Alberto,

731
00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:56.460
like it was this this kind of expectation of privacy

732
00:32:57.600 --> 00:32:59.460
that in fact was not private at all,

733
00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:02.810
it at all funneled back to Salazar,

734
00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:05.410
again, him being a puppeteer,

735
00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:08.330
and controlling the flow of information between everybody.

736
00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:13.330
Yes and I came up in a household where there was a doctor,

737
00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:16.240
and somebody who really prided themselves

738
00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:17.920
on being a good doctor,

739
00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:21.400
and being somebody who never overstepped the rules

740
00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.530
or things like patient client privilege

741
00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:25.960
is incredibly important.

742
00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:29.870
And so, when you almost are exposed to that at a young age,

743
00:33:29.870 --> 00:33:32.800
and then you're told somebody is a sports psychologist,

744
00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:33.633
I know what that means,

745
00:33:33.633 --> 00:33:36.080
and all the other athletes know what that mean.

746
00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.080
And so, if they're telling something personal or private,

747
00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:44.930
they're going to assume that

748
00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:47.480
it's not gonna be told to somebody else,

749
00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:50.040
let alone their competitor teammate,

750
00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:52.603
or their coach who maybe they're talking about.

751
00:33:53.670 --> 00:33:56.940
And over time, I really,

752
00:33:56.940 --> 00:33:59.450
I think the thing I regret the most is that

753
00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:02.920
there were moments where I realized what was happening

754
00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:08.000
in that another athlete would be discussed with me

755
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.830
who I knew he worked with,

756
00:34:09.830 --> 00:34:12.233
and in a way that was incredibly negative,

757
00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:15.420
but rather than take that moment

758
00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:16.487
and stepping back and saying,

759
00:34:16.487 --> 00:34:18.350
"Hey, this is inappropriate.

760
00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:20.330
He's hypothetically breaking laws

761
00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:22.390
if he's truly a sports psychologist,

762
00:34:22.390 --> 00:34:24.140
what is he doing to me?"

763
00:34:24.140 --> 00:34:26.237
Instead in those moments, I was just like,

764
00:34:26.237 --> 00:34:28.730
"I'm glad he's not talking about me, or my weight,

765
00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:30.340
or saying something mean about me."

766
00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:32.900
Yeah, I think the thing that people don't get,

767
00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:35.670
or might miss in trying to understand that

768
00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:36.910
this dynamic is that

769
00:34:36.910 --> 00:34:39.700
when you're an athlete at the highest level,

770
00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:41.650
and you're on this elite team,

771
00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:42.900
there's a couple of things at play.

772
00:34:42.900 --> 00:34:44.500
One of which is

773
00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:47.040
everybody's trying to covet favor with the coach,

774
00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:49.210
everybody wants to be the favorite.

775
00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:53.050
You hear, Kara Goucher has spoken about this with Alberta,

776
00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:54.870
and when you're in that catbird seat

777
00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:56.420
and you are the favorite,

778
00:34:56.420 --> 00:34:58.030
it doesn't matter what the coach is saying

779
00:34:58.030 --> 00:35:01.510
about the other athletes because you're gonna protect that.

780
00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:03.610
And just because you're all on the same team,

781
00:35:03.610 --> 00:35:06.310
there isn't the esprit de corps that you would suspect

782
00:35:07.834 --> 00:35:09.140
because everybody's vying

783
00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:11.223
for a limited number of Olympic slots,

784
00:35:11.223 --> 00:35:12.910
these may be your teammates

785
00:35:12.910 --> 00:35:14.170
and you're training with them every day,

786
00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:16.220
and on some level, some surface level,

787
00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:17.700
you're mutually supportive,

788
00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:21.250
but ultimately they're your competitors also.

789
00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:23.300
So there's a cutthroat aspect of this.

790
00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:25.230
So it's working at cross purposes

791
00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:26.917
with the kind of unity that you would want

792
00:35:26.917 --> 00:35:30.150
with the people you're showing up to train with every day.

793
00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:31.400
Yeah, absolutely.

794
00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:33.760
And I think that's why personally

795
00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:37.010
I've never named other athletes.

796
00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:39.430
Even in stories that I've told

797
00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:42.060
in which I'm referring to another teammate of mine

798
00:35:42.060 --> 00:35:46.730
doing something negative, or being in the room or et cetera,

799
00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:51.520
I don't name them because personally although I've never,

800
00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:53.170
minus a couple of individuals,

801
00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:55.467
I've never gotten really like a,

802
00:35:55.467 --> 00:35:58.493
"Hey, I heard you," I'm sorry, moment,

803
00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:02.050
I don't need that from them.

804
00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:06.180
And even though I think their behavior was bad, and poor,

805
00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:11.180
and the inability for people to stand up after the fact

806
00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:14.450
and say like, "Yes, that was wrong,"

807
00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:16.550
is something that I'm always gonna wonder,

808
00:36:18.363 --> 00:36:19.847
it takes a lot of bravery to do that,

809
00:36:19.847 --> 00:36:23.030
and it takes a lot of vulnerability to do that,

810
00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:28.030
but it's my maybe ability to somewhat sympathize

811
00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:29.840
is the reason that I don't.

812
00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:33.070
I almost try to keep the athletes as out of this as I can.

813
00:36:33.070 --> 00:36:34.780
Yeah, I mean,

814
00:36:34.780 --> 00:36:36.530
and listen, we should point out that

815
00:36:36.530 --> 00:36:38.270
in the wake of you sharing your story

816
00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:40.270
with "The New York Times,"

817
00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:41.767
so many people came out to support you,

818
00:36:41.767 --> 00:36:44.310
and all these people that were on your team

819
00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:48.440
have come out of the woodwork to say nice things about you,

820
00:36:48.440 --> 00:36:52.220
and also express their regret at not having been

821
00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:55.300
kind of more aware or supportive in the moment.

822
00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:57.860
Yeah, and I think that's why it's like

823
00:36:57.860 --> 00:37:01.040
both Cam Levins and Dathan Ritzenhein,

824
00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:02.963
were my two athletes at the time,

825
00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:06.420
who have since reached out to me,

826
00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:10.260
and publicly said they witnessed,

827
00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:12.490
or at least in the case of Cam,

828
00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:14.850
witnessed some of this behavior,

829
00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:19.697
is something that I always cry when I talk about that

830
00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:23.120
'cause I think again,

831
00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:25.883
for so many years when you think it's your fault,

832
00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:30.923
having at least one person say they thought,

833
00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:34.163
that's always the thing that kills me.

834
00:37:36.862 --> 00:37:38.250
And I think it's just because

835
00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:42.890
I know how brave it is to say I messed up,

836
00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:43.820
I know how brave it is

837
00:37:43.820 --> 00:37:45.820
to say I wish I did something different.

838
00:37:46.940 --> 00:37:51.940
And there are people I wish that I had saved over the years,

839
00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:57.580
and so, to have somebody come out and say

840
00:37:57.580 --> 00:37:58.820
that really means a lot.

841
00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:00.020
So, I always-

842
00:38:00.020 --> 00:38:00.970
Especially

843
00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:06.470
if and when they're still part of the Nike family,

844
00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:10.480
there's a lot of stake, it's risky to do that and so,

845
00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:13.630
yeah, that is something to be appreciated,

846
00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:14.600
and it's very cool.

847
00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:19.260
And in terms of you thinking perhaps

848
00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:21.820
some of these situations alluded your gaze,

849
00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:24.890
you're now in this incredible situation

850
00:38:24.890 --> 00:38:26.610
where you can be this voice for change.

851
00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:29.110
And I know you've taken that mantle seriously,

852
00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:31.050
and we're gonna get into that.

853
00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:33.610
But when I think about Alexi,

854
00:38:33.610 --> 00:38:37.800
I see somebody who really appreciates

855
00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:41.440
and understands the power of mentorship.

856
00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:43.870
She's always seeking out mentors,

857
00:38:43.870 --> 00:38:47.930
and she understands her responsibility and role as a mentor.

858
00:38:47.930 --> 00:38:51.800
And on some level that's part of your friendship dynamic

859
00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:54.630
because she's a little bit further down the path

860
00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:55.750
and all of that.

861
00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:58.610
And now because of your relationship with Alexi

862
00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:00.370
and what you've gone through,

863
00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:03.120
I feel like you really get that as well.

864
00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:03.970
100%.

865
00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:06.913
And I think for me as a very young athlete,

866
00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:09.880
one thing that's always a little awkward,

867
00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:13.850
and I'm sure everybody who's ever been kind of on that,

868
00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:16.563
for lack of a better word phenom level,

869
00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:20.990
you kind of expect everybody to welcome you with open arms

870
00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:24.030
'cause you're like, "I'm 12, this is fun.

871
00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:26.330
Everybody's gonna be my friend."

872
00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:31.330
And the truth is you're often met with a lot of negativity,

873
00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:34.380
especially within the competitive space.

874
00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:37.530
And I get it, if you're a 30 year old competitor,

875
00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:40.080
you don't really want this 18 year old beating you.

876
00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:46.280
But I think kind of having felt like I was alone

877
00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:48.703
for so long in sport,

878
00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:52.823
and was able to find a friendship like Alexi's.

879
00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:56.560
She was one of the first people who really

880
00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:57.550
took me under their wing,

881
00:39:57.550 --> 00:40:00.600
and shared advice, and shared stories,

882
00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:03.100
and who didn't just

883
00:40:03.100 --> 00:40:05.210
kind of look at me as purely a competitor,

884
00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:08.713
or purely somebody to use for pacing or whatever.

885
00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:12.710
And as a result now I'm very cognizant

886
00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:14.100
of the younger generation,

887
00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:15.450
and the people who are coming up,

888
00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.930
and after a race,

889
00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:19.930
they're gonna be the first people I'd be aligned over to

890
00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:23.150
so that I can invite them to dinner, or hanging out with or,

891
00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:25.380
just make them feel like they were a part of the event

892
00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:26.803
in a way that matters.

893
00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:29.970
And it's sad 'cause even to this day

894
00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:32.720
I haven't been to too many races,

895
00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:35.360
there've been a lot of injuries over the years.

896
00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:38.150
But the ones that I am, it's like go over to somebody,

897
00:40:38.150 --> 00:40:40.450
I'm like, "Why is nobody else doing this too?"

898
00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:45.480
And I think it's because sport in so many ways has been,

899
00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:47.980
at least on the professional side,

900
00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:49.750
the number of times I'll listen to an interview

901
00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:53.800
and an athlete will say like, "Well, sport is selfish,"

902
00:40:53.800 --> 00:40:55.970
and I'm like, "I think that's a cop out.

903
00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:58.630
I think it's selfish if you want it to be selfish."

904
00:40:58.630 --> 00:41:03.260
If you wanna kind of not give back in some way,

905
00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:04.773
then you can choose not to.

906
00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:08.850
But if you wanna have a purpose kind of during your career,

907
00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:11.100
and do something during it,

908
00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:13.080
there's always gonna be opportunity to.

909
00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.640
Yeah, well, I think people are wired in different ways.

910
00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:18.320
There are certain competitors who feel like

911
00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:20.420
that's the only path to greatness

912
00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:23.540
is if they're completely focused on themselves.

913
00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:27.300
Any athlete who's excelled with longevity though,

914
00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:30.540
realizes that that's an unsustainable fuel source,

915
00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:34.550
and ultimately leads to early retirement or burnout.

916
00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:37.340
And the true greats at some point,

917
00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:39.320
even if they had that mindset earlier,

918
00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:41.650
realize that when you're living,

919
00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:43.430
when you broaden the aperture on your life,

920
00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:45.740
that actually your performance gets better.

921
00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:48.330
I mean, Shalane talks about that quite a bit as well.

922
00:41:48.330 --> 00:41:50.290
Like when she's having kids and all of that

923
00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:51.890
and trying to juggle you think,

924
00:41:51.890 --> 00:41:53.510
or Kerri Walsh Jennings, right?

925
00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:57.210
She's married with kids and trying to juggle that,

926
00:41:57.210 --> 00:41:59.240
and saying, "Oh, it actually helps me.

927
00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:01.060
It makes me a better competitor."

928
00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:03.010
But I think at the same time,

929
00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:08.010
there's a systemic misalignment of incentives in that

930
00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:13.100
the sponsorship ecosystem is set up

931
00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:18.100
to financially reward you for your performance, right?

932
00:42:18.450 --> 00:42:21.230
And this is something you're very active and upending,

933
00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:24.140
but it's not just, "Hey, there's only a few slots

934
00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:26.620
to make the world championship team or the Olympic team,"

935
00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:27.537
it's that,

936
00:42:27.537 --> 00:42:30.990
"My bank account is contingent upon me beating you,

937
00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:33.890
and if I get beat by you,

938
00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:36.850
my shoe sponsor may not renew my contract."

939
00:42:36.850 --> 00:42:41.600
So you're living event to event and kind of in fear

940
00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:45.280
of trying to protect your ability to put food on the table.

941
00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:49.460
And so, that's gonna create a negative environment

942
00:42:49.460 --> 00:42:51.890
in terms of how the athletes are

943
00:42:51.890 --> 00:42:53.150
interacting with each other.

944
00:42:53.150 --> 00:42:53.983
Absolutely.

945
00:42:53.983 --> 00:42:56.970
And it's going to create the win at all costs culture,

946
00:42:56.970 --> 00:43:01.663
and honestly predispose people to cheat.

947
00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:04.779
And it's because I get it.

948
00:43:04.779 --> 00:43:09.540
If that's your only financial opportunity,

949
00:43:09.540 --> 00:43:11.963
and it's all about competition.

950
00:43:13.963 --> 00:43:16.400
And I think the unfortunate thing too is

951
00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:18.970
who gets a contract versus who doesn't?

952
00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:22.000
Yes, it's all about performance,

953
00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:25.380
but if you look the right way,

954
00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:27.620
maybe we're gonna knock you a couple of extra dollars,

955
00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:31.820
or if we just like the way you interview,

956
00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:34.280
there's no real metric.

957
00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:36.290
Even when you look at the disparity

958
00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:38.020
between different people's contracts

959
00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:41.683
because everything is NDA'd, and so nobody,

960
00:43:42.770 --> 00:43:43.640
if you're in the sports world-

961
00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:45.150
Yeah, there's no transparency about what they,

962
00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:48.050
so the athletes don't know who's getting what.

963
00:43:48.050 --> 00:43:51.740
Exactly, and some people might be open

964
00:43:51.740 --> 00:43:52.950
and share with others,

965
00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:55.710
and other times it's very tight lipped.

966
00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:59.830
And the problem is you'll know one person's making

967
00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:03.500
below minimum wage to compete at really the highest level,

968
00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:05.883
and somebody else is doing incredibly well.

969
00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:09.930
And even the incredibly well side it's just,

970
00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:12.723
there's not a lot of money in track and field.

971
00:44:13.910 --> 00:44:17.830
And I think when athletes aren't able

972
00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:19.660
to really negotiate for themselves

973
00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:21.040
when they're not even aware of

974
00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:23.873
what's a good price to be negotiating,

975
00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:26.720
it leaves just so much agency

976
00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:28.370
out of the hands of the athletes.

977
00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:34.240
I feel like the sponsorship approach

978
00:44:34.240 --> 00:44:36.900
that a lot of these brands have historically taken

979
00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:40.370
is really based on antiquated metrics.

980
00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:41.203
The idea like,

981
00:44:41.203 --> 00:44:43.860
"Oh, you have to make this team and go to the,

982
00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:45.640
these are the events you're gonna show up at,

983
00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:48.560
and if you get first, you get this, second, third,"

984
00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:52.600
all of that, and that is dictating how much money you make

985
00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:54.220
really doesn't make sense,

986
00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:56.830
especially in a social media world.

987
00:44:56.830 --> 00:45:00.780
What is the purpose of a company sponsoring an athlete,

988
00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:03.150
it's to engender goodwill in the brand,

989
00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:07.200
and hopefully encourage people out in the world

990
00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:08.550
to purchase their products.

991
00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:09.770
Well, how do you do that?

992
00:45:09.770 --> 00:45:12.290
Well, winning races is just one way,

993
00:45:12.290 --> 00:45:14.210
but in an era where

994
00:45:14.210 --> 00:45:16.480
athletes are shouldering the responsibility

995
00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:18.850
of communicating directly with audiences,

996
00:45:18.850 --> 00:45:22.643
and developing their own trust with those people,

997
00:45:23.640 --> 00:45:24.473
it's not,

998
00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:28.370
where are you stand on the podium

999
00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:30.780
no longer is really the most important thing.

1000
00:45:30.780 --> 00:45:33.350
It's who do people like?

1001
00:45:33.350 --> 00:45:35.130
Who do people trust?

1002
00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:39.180
That could be somebody who's not making any of these meets

1003
00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:41.140
or these competitions,

1004
00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:42.930
and somebody who no one's ever heard of

1005
00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:45.450
that has no audience and no interest

1006
00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:48.080
in communicating with the public in any way,

1007
00:45:48.080 --> 00:45:51.380
and wins, I don't know, the steeple chase

1008
00:45:51.380 --> 00:45:53.950
or something like that in some European race,

1009
00:45:53.950 --> 00:45:57.480
is that moving the needle in terms of the brand?

1010
00:45:57.480 --> 00:45:58.920
So it just doesn't,

1011
00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:02.950
it needs to be completely rethought bottom to top.

1012
00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:04.350
And that's why I love

1013
00:46:04.350 --> 00:46:06.550
kind of this relationship that you have with Tracksmith,

1014
00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:09.697
you're just basically a salaried employee and they're like,

1015
00:46:09.697 --> 00:46:11.340
"We love you, go do your thing,

1016
00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:15.710
and spread the love of running, and yeah, go do some races,

1017
00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:18.810
but where you end up in those races

1018
00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:22.140
really is not relevant to your paycheck."

1019
00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:23.277
Yeah, no, not at all,

1020
00:46:23.277 --> 00:46:24.310
and if anything,

1021
00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:27.070
I think they value my performance as an employee

1022
00:46:27.070 --> 00:46:27.953
more than an athlete.

1023
00:46:27.953 --> 00:46:30.370
I think if I was dropping the ball,

1024
00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:33.250
and missing all of my assignments,

1025
00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:35.020
I think we'd have real talking to,

1026
00:46:35.020 --> 00:46:37.860
even if I made the Olympic team tomorrow.

1027
00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:40.470
And I think that's incredibly important

1028
00:46:40.470 --> 00:46:43.490
because it's a way to value athletes

1029
00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:45.750
as more than just products.

1030
00:46:45.750 --> 00:46:48.550
It's a way for me to develop a career skill

1031
00:46:48.550 --> 00:46:52.800
that long-term is transferable into the open market

1032
00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:54.887
because as much as I love running

1033
00:46:54.887 --> 00:46:57.710
and as much as we all love sport,

1034
00:46:57.710 --> 00:47:01.150
everybody has a point where

1035
00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:02.940
they're going to have to move on to their next career,

1036
00:47:02.940 --> 00:47:07.940
and the truth is no runner is really getting set up for life

1037
00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:09.300
based on their salary.

1038
00:47:09.300 --> 00:47:12.210
It's not like we're in a professional sports league

1039
00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.000
where if you make it to 40,

1040
00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:17.223
you're retiring in a really great natural spot.

1041
00:47:18.490 --> 00:47:20.340
And as a result, there's kind of this

1042
00:47:21.180 --> 00:47:24.510
inability for most people in these traditional contracts

1043
00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:27.570
to develop those career skills in a way that's

1044
00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:30.900
as supportive as a relationship like Tracksmith that I have

1045
00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:35.530
where my training, my dreams as an athlete

1046
00:47:35.530 --> 00:47:38.040
are beyond supported.

1047
00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:41.167
And if one day I'm not dialing in until 10:30

1048
00:47:41.167 --> 00:47:42.480
'cause I had a little bit more

1049
00:47:42.480 --> 00:47:44.290
of an important workout in the morning,

1050
00:47:44.290 --> 00:47:47.540
so as a result I'm gonna be sending some emails at 7:00 p.m.

1051
00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:48.940
that's totally understood.

1052
00:47:48.940 --> 00:47:52.510
And that's encouraged, that's accepted,

1053
00:47:52.510 --> 00:47:54.710
and it's not something that I almost have to

1054
00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:59.080
combine two incompatible worlds.

1055
00:47:59.080 --> 00:48:00.830
And I think a lot of other athletes

1056
00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:02.900
who've had to kind of dive into the workforce

1057
00:48:02.900 --> 00:48:05.923
while sustaining their careers for that financial support,

1058
00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:09.040
it can be harder if you're just in a traditional company

1059
00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:11.440
that maybe doesn't understand why

1060
00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:14.940
you have to travel X amount of times to go to meets,

1061
00:48:14.940 --> 00:48:16.437
and kind of random parts of the country where they're like-

1062
00:48:16.437 --> 00:48:18.620
Yeah, and it's expensive.

1063
00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:21.740
Yeah, but if you look at other different kinds of sports

1064
00:48:21.740 --> 00:48:25.270
and certain legacy brands like in the climbing world,

1065
00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:26.620
in the mountaineering world,

1066
00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:31.300
you look at Salomon, North Face,

1067
00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:34.250
and athletes like Rebecca Rusch, and Hilaree Nelson,

1068
00:48:34.250 --> 00:48:36.750
Conrad Anker, Alex Honnold,

1069
00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.340
Ricky Gates, Kilian Jornet,

1070
00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:42.580
these brands are on board with these athletes

1071
00:48:42.580 --> 00:48:45.980
for their life, it's like, "Go be awesome.

1072
00:48:45.980 --> 00:48:47.360
Just go out and do cool stuff,

1073
00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:48.680
and we're here to support you."

1074
00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:51.070
And people love those brands as a result,

1075
00:48:51.070 --> 00:48:51.903
it just seems better.

1076
00:48:51.903 --> 00:48:54.580
Obviously, look Kilian and Ricky,

1077
00:48:54.580 --> 00:48:57.110
they do interesting different types of things in running,

1078
00:48:57.110 --> 00:48:59.310
it's not track and field where there's a stopwatch,

1079
00:48:59.310 --> 00:49:00.750
and it's really about metrics.

1080
00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:03.930
But I think there's something to be learned

1081
00:49:03.930 --> 00:49:05.160
from those brands

1082
00:49:05.160 --> 00:49:07.170
who have been doing it for a very long time.

1083
00:49:07.170 --> 00:49:09.470
And now other sports are trying to catch up to that.

1084
00:49:09.470 --> 00:49:10.910
I mean, surfing, it's the same thing

1085
00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:13.700
with surfers and all the kind of surf brands.

1086
00:49:13.700 --> 00:49:14.533
Absolutely.

1087
00:49:14.533 --> 00:49:16.300
And I think one thing a lot of these sports have in common

1088
00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:18.450
is that they're all lifestyle sports

1089
00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:20.500
in that a lot of the kind of like

1090
00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:22.210
traditional professional sports

1091
00:49:22.210 --> 00:49:23.750
that we think of in the United States,

1092
00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:26.430
at least like football, basketball,

1093
00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:29.850
yes, you can continue doing them post collegiately,

1094
00:49:29.850 --> 00:49:34.037
but there's this almost understanding that like,

1095
00:49:34.037 --> 00:49:35.500
"You're not going to be in the NFL,"

1096
00:49:35.500 --> 00:49:37.617
or there's kind of this separation where like,

1097
00:49:37.617 --> 00:49:39.720
"I probably can't play again,

1098
00:49:39.720 --> 00:49:41.700
unless we're doing a pickup game,

1099
00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:44.040
or I really assembled 20 people.

1100
00:49:44.040 --> 00:49:45.980
It's probably harder to join a league. "

1101
00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:47.210
It's a lot of,

1102
00:49:47.210 --> 00:49:49.820
it almost takes a lot of scrappiness and ingenuity

1103
00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:52.290
to make sure you can keep playing.

1104
00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:56.140
But sports like surfing, rock climbing, running,

1105
00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:57.620
there's no barrier.

1106
00:49:57.620 --> 00:50:02.290
There's no, "Hey, I need to find six guys to play with.

1107
00:50:02.290 --> 00:50:03.430
And even then

1108
00:50:03.430 --> 00:50:06.260
we need to find another six guys to play against."

1109
00:50:06.260 --> 00:50:10.333
You can just lease a $80 pairs of shoes and go for a run.

1110
00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:13.710
And I think kind of having that opportunity

1111
00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:18.710
to really touch so many more people makes the idea of this,

1112
00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:22.040
the way we're kind of talking about these,

1113
00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:25.200
umbrella them into these little big sports,

1114
00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:27.340
really should lean more towards the

1115
00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:31.340
how are you talking to that every day athlete?

1116
00:50:31.340 --> 00:50:33.690
Because there's so many more of them out there.

1117
00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:37.830
And it's not to say that you have to talk to them,

1118
00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:41.230
and therefore dumbed down how you're talking about running,

1119
00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:45.910
or almost make it something where it's like

1120
00:50:45.910 --> 00:50:49.300
you're not almost taking the running side of it seriously,

1121
00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:51.690
but instead you can lift all those people up with you

1122
00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:52.523
and say like,

1123
00:50:52.523 --> 00:50:54.900
"Hey, I think you deserve to know how to recover

1124
00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:55.770
like I recover.

1125
00:50:55.770 --> 00:50:58.910
I think you deserve to know how to train like I train,

1126
00:50:58.910 --> 00:51:03.430
and we can help you come up with ways to

1127
00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:05.680
build that into your life, and your schedule,

1128
00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:10.680
and just almost create this incredibly supportive community

1129
00:51:11.170 --> 00:51:12.370
that I think in a way

1130
00:51:12.370 --> 00:51:15.460
a lot of the traditional professional sports

1131
00:51:15.460 --> 00:51:18.620
kind of don't have the opportunity to tap into.

1132
00:51:18.620 --> 00:51:21.260
Well, it's purely based on inspiration.

1133
00:51:21.260 --> 00:51:26.090
You have the LeBrons and people like that that are just gods

1134
00:51:26.090 --> 00:51:27.440
that we look up to.

1135
00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.060
And if they say buy this product,

1136
00:51:29.060 --> 00:51:31.040
we're expected to buy it, right?

1137
00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:35.423
The difference that you're speaking to is being,

1138
00:51:38.330 --> 00:51:41.060
yes, you're inspirational, but you didn't go to the Olympics

1139
00:51:41.060 --> 00:51:43.070
and win five gold medals, right?

1140
00:51:43.070 --> 00:51:45.070
You're a much more accessible human being.

1141
00:51:45.070 --> 00:51:47.610
And so, you're more aspirational.

1142
00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:51.240
People aren't gonna be able to run as fast as Mary Cain,

1143
00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:53.470
but, "I like Mary Cain, and she's cool,

1144
00:51:53.470 --> 00:51:55.080
and she's gonna come out and run with us,

1145
00:51:55.080 --> 00:51:57.570
and she's going to help us along the way,"

1146
00:51:57.570 --> 00:52:00.350
there's a relationship aspect of it.

1147
00:52:00.350 --> 00:52:02.490
It's not just somebody on the billboard that

1148
00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:04.790
if you saw them in person, you would freak out.

1149
00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:06.590
Exactly, and I think the truth is

1150
00:52:06.590 --> 00:52:10.040
even the athletes who win five Olympic gold medals,

1151
00:52:10.040 --> 00:52:12.960
can still almost do that in running

1152
00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:14.550
compared to other sports.

1153
00:52:14.550 --> 00:52:16.680
And it's because at the end of the day

1154
00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:20.650
running is something that you don't need

1155
00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:24.590
that much extra stuff to do, right?

1156
00:52:24.590 --> 00:52:26.730
If you have a road, if you have a trail,

1157
00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:28.930
if you have a track and,

1158
00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:31.690
some people don't even wear a pair of shoes,

1159
00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:33.260
you can go out and run.

1160
00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:36.520
And the sort of training I'm doing versus somebody else,

1161
00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:38.680
yeah, you might not run as much,

1162
00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:40.840
maybe the times are gonna be different,

1163
00:52:40.840 --> 00:52:43.410
but you could still go out there and do an interval workout.

1164
00:52:43.410 --> 00:52:46.350
You could still go out there and run a long run,

1165
00:52:46.350 --> 00:52:49.480
and just build it to what works for you.

1166
00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:53.000
But whether you're the Olympic champion 20 times,

1167
00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:55.340
or this is your first time running,

1168
00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:57.510
you really have something in common,

1169
00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:00.913
and there's this ability to, I believe connect,

1170
00:53:02.050 --> 00:53:05.400
in a way that's just really not,

1171
00:53:05.400 --> 00:53:08.130
or really overlooked right now in an unfortunate way.

1172
00:53:08.130 --> 00:53:10.020
Yeah, well, you're also running around New York City

1173
00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:11.530
just everybody else, right?

1174
00:53:11.530 --> 00:53:13.680
Running is one of the few sports where

1175
00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:15.720
track and field is different, but road running,

1176
00:53:15.720 --> 00:53:19.270
trail running, the elites are running the same course

1177
00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:21.880
at essentially the same time as everybody else.

1178
00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:25.203
So it gives it a different vibe entirely.

1179
00:53:26.450 --> 00:53:29.750
And I think Tracksmith is this great

1180
00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:31.330
kind of platform for you to do that,

1181
00:53:31.330 --> 00:53:34.540
but I wanna talk about Atalanta.

1182
00:53:34.540 --> 00:53:35.460
How do you say it?
Atalanta.

1183
00:53:35.460 --> 00:53:37.180
I wanna know where this word came from,

1184
00:53:37.180 --> 00:53:42.070
but because you're really inverting the whole business model

1185
00:53:42.070 --> 00:53:44.690
with what you're doing with this new team.

1186
00:53:44.690 --> 00:53:46.670
Yeah, so I think just to start off,

1187
00:53:46.670 --> 00:53:49.710
I'll maybe explain the name which is,

1188
00:53:49.710 --> 00:53:54.010
in sixth grade, being the nerd that I am, I took Latin.

1189
00:53:54.010 --> 00:53:56.490
And at the very beginning of the year

1190
00:53:56.490 --> 00:54:00.360
we had to pick a mythological name,

1191
00:54:00.360 --> 00:54:02.140
and that would be the name we'd be called

1192
00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:03.140
throughout the year.

1193
00:54:04.099 --> 00:54:06.070
And at the time I was not a runner.

1194
00:54:06.070 --> 00:54:09.650
I had run a 5:47 mile as a sixth grader just as

1195
00:54:09.650 --> 00:54:11.403
an afterschool gym thing.
Right.

1196
00:54:11.403 --> 00:54:15.060
I'm not a runner. I just ran 5:47 in sixth grade.

1197
00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:16.160
What?
Yes.

1198
00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:19.363
So I acknowledge that into much just to say-

1199
00:54:19.363 --> 00:54:21.920
I take back all the stuff about you being relatable,

1200
00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:22.753
but go ahead.

1201
00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:26.530
When I went to my dad who had taken

1202
00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:28.427
kind of years of Latin himself and I was like,

1203
00:54:28.427 --> 00:54:31.420
"Oh, what should be the name I take?"

1204
00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:32.817
He immediately was like,

1205
00:54:32.817 --> 00:54:35.500
"That 5:47 let's do Atalanta."

1206
00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:39.920
And the reason is it's this really beautiful Greek myth

1207
00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:44.920
about a woman who is a human being, they're not a goddess,

1208
00:54:45.140 --> 00:54:48.830
but they're the fastest being in the world.

1209
00:54:48.830 --> 00:54:50.980
And they're faster than all men,

1210
00:54:50.980 --> 00:54:54.070
they're faster than all the gods and the goddesses.

1211
00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:59.070
And so much of their myth is this more feminist based myth.

1212
00:55:00.230 --> 00:55:01.510
I mean, as much as they go.

1213
00:55:01.510 --> 00:55:03.390
Like a "La Strada" kind of thing.

1214
00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:08.390
Yes, where it's like she is a warrior, an athlete-

1215
00:55:08.650 --> 00:55:10.472
Right, like from the "Wonder Woman" Island.

1216
00:55:10.472 --> 00:55:12.070
Exactly.

1217
00:55:12.070 --> 00:55:13.270
What about Achilles?

1218
00:55:13.270 --> 00:55:15.500
They just kicked him to the curb?

1219
00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:17.263
She's faster.
She's faster.

1220
00:55:18.220 --> 00:55:20.910
And so, I always love that,

1221
00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:25.170
for seven years when I took Latin, that was my Latin name.

1222
00:55:25.170 --> 00:55:27.110
And it was just one of those stories that always

1223
00:55:27.110 --> 00:55:29.263
was kind of in the back of my head.

1224
00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:33.120
And then recently when I was going on this endeavor

1225
00:55:33.120 --> 00:55:34.980
to start a program

1226
00:55:34.980 --> 00:55:37.070
I mentioned like, "Oh, we're coming up with names,"

1227
00:55:37.070 --> 00:55:38.730
and I threw that out there

1228
00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.710
to one of my coworkers in New York Road Runners,

1229
00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:41.870
and it turns out

1230
00:55:41.870 --> 00:55:45.760
the first ever women's high-performance team in the US

1231
00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:48.130
was based in New York City in the 1970s

1232
00:55:48.130 --> 00:55:49.928
and that was their name.
Wow. That's cool.

1233
00:55:49.928 --> 00:55:51.987
And I immediately was like,

1234
00:55:51.987 --> 00:55:54.970
"There's never really been a professional team in New York,

1235
00:55:54.970 --> 00:55:57.507
but that is the closest thing that's come to it."

1236
00:55:57.507 --> 00:55:59.820
And I'm like, "Let's bring it back."

1237
00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:02.470
Wow, I just got goosebumps. That's very cool.

1238
00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:05.720
Thank you.
And so this is set up,

1239
00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:07.350
this is the other interesting thing here.

1240
00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:10.060
You've set this up as a nonprofit.

1241
00:56:10.060 --> 00:56:12.110
So it's a nonprofit, but then,

1242
00:56:12.110 --> 00:56:14.510
and it's got kind of two prongs as I understand it.

1243
00:56:14.510 --> 00:56:17.480
There's going to be this elite running team

1244
00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:20.110
of six to eight athletes,

1245
00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:22.290
and then there's a whole community arm

1246
00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:23.860
that entails the outreach,

1247
00:56:23.860 --> 00:56:26.690
and getting kids on the track and all of that.

1248
00:56:26.690 --> 00:56:28.690
But, the professional athletes

1249
00:56:28.690 --> 00:56:30.350
that are gonna comprise this team

1250
00:56:30.350 --> 00:56:34.240
are gonna be paid employees of the nonprofit.

1251
00:56:34.240 --> 00:56:37.229
That's never been done before, that's super interesting.

1252
00:56:37.229 --> 00:56:41.180
Yeah, so one thing that I'll immediately call out is that

1253
00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:43.110
certain teams across the country,

1254
00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:47.280
in Olympic movement sports are registered as non-profits.

1255
00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:51.760
And the reason is, is the Amateur Athlete Act of some year

1256
00:56:51.760 --> 00:56:55.320
made it so that that was an opportunity.

1257
00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:59.270
But we're not a non-profit in name only, we're a nonprofit

1258
00:56:59.270 --> 00:57:00.520
and that we're actually gonna be doing

1259
00:57:00.520 --> 00:57:02.053
service work in the community.

1260
00:57:03.010 --> 00:57:07.160
And in many ways I had just always seen this problem

1261
00:57:07.160 --> 00:57:10.640
of the elite and of sport in wanting to solve it.

1262
00:57:10.640 --> 00:57:12.320
And my relationship with Tracksmith

1263
00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:14.220
was one in which I realized

1264
00:57:14.220 --> 00:57:18.380
giving athletes a full time employment opportunity

1265
00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:20.010
where they're building career skills,

1266
00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:21.630
taking on an actual job

1267
00:57:22.490 --> 00:57:25.050
while still being fully supported to train,

1268
00:57:25.050 --> 00:57:26.690
is an incredible model,

1269
00:57:26.690 --> 00:57:29.347
and one in which I was always trying to figure out like,

1270
00:57:29.347 --> 00:57:31.260
"How can I grow this?

1271
00:57:31.260 --> 00:57:33.577
How can I give more people this opportunity?"

1272
00:57:34.620 --> 00:57:36.980
But I think the thing that kept kind of

1273
00:57:36.980 --> 00:57:39.100
sticking in the back of my head is

1274
00:57:39.100 --> 00:57:42.220
it will be great to help the professional side of sport,

1275
00:57:42.220 --> 00:57:43.940
but how can I make this bigger?

1276
00:57:43.940 --> 00:57:45.980
How can I help people who maybe

1277
00:57:45.980 --> 00:57:48.253
never aspire to go on the professional end?

1278
00:57:49.150 --> 00:57:51.740
And to me, that was giving back to

1279
00:57:51.740 --> 00:57:53.940
younger athletes in the local community

1280
00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:59.130
who just maybe have been left behind in terms of programming

1281
00:57:59.130 --> 00:58:01.670
to get them into sport, and stay at sport,

1282
00:58:01.670 --> 00:58:04.970
and not only do it for this performance end,

1283
00:58:04.970 --> 00:58:08.440
but learn all of the skills around healthy sport,

1284
00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.300
and keeping a positive mindset.

1285
00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:12.980
And a lot of the things that

1286
00:58:12.980 --> 00:58:15.870
maybe as a young girl had I learned,

1287
00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:20.330
would have helped me maintain a more positive relationship

1288
00:58:20.330 --> 00:58:25.330
with sport forever when instead I kind of ended up falling

1289
