1545
01:10:00.330 --> 01:10:02.710
and I didn't have my period between the ages of

1546
01:10:02.710 --> 01:10:04.403
18 and 21 and a half.

1547
01:10:05.950 --> 01:10:08.195
Yeah, it's so pretty bad.

1548
01:10:08.195 --> 01:10:10.200
Yeah.

1549
01:10:10.200 --> 01:10:11.500
It reminds me,

1550
01:10:11.500 --> 01:10:15.150
I spoke about this with Lauren Fleshman,

1551
01:10:15.150 --> 01:10:16.603
who I know you know,

1552
01:10:17.670 --> 01:10:19.623
and it really speaks to,

1553
01:10:21.080 --> 01:10:24.130
again, this systemic issue of

1554
01:10:24.130 --> 01:10:26.740
this kind of male dominated paradigm,

1555
01:10:26.740 --> 01:10:30.300
where the men who are the support system and the coaches

1556
01:10:30.300 --> 01:10:33.880
are training women fundamentally like men,

1557
01:10:33.880 --> 01:10:37.540
and built into that is this assumption that

1558
01:10:37.540 --> 01:10:41.200
that women's bodies adapt and change

1559
01:10:41.200 --> 01:10:44.670
in the same way that dudes do, and it's just not the case.

1560
01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:48.630
The way that the female body sort of matures over time

1561
01:10:48.630 --> 01:10:49.840
is extremely different.

1562
01:10:49.840 --> 01:10:52.107
You were talking about, yeah, when you're a girl,

1563
01:10:52.107 --> 01:10:54.200
and you're starting to put on a little bit of weight,

1564
01:10:54.200 --> 01:10:55.163
that's normal.

1565
01:10:56.570 --> 01:10:59.650
But that hammer to just always keep your weight down,

1566
01:10:59.650 --> 01:11:01.240
always keep your weight down,

1567
01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:06.160
leading people into this situation of disordered eating.

1568
01:11:06.160 --> 01:11:08.750
I mean, it's really a big problem.

1569
01:11:08.750 --> 01:11:10.250
Yeah, and I think

1570
01:11:10.250 --> 01:11:13.700
a big thing I always like to start off saying is that

1571
01:11:13.700 --> 01:11:15.250
whenever I say women's training

1572
01:11:15.250 --> 01:11:16.700
needs to be different than men,

1573
01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:18.810
we can train as hard as guys,

1574
01:11:18.810 --> 01:11:21.700
if anything, I think the women can train harder.

1575
01:11:21.700 --> 01:11:24.450
Some of the workouts I've seen people do,

1576
01:11:24.450 --> 01:11:25.503
we can grind.

1577
01:11:26.490 --> 01:11:29.190
But there are certain things that are different

1578
01:11:29.190 --> 01:11:30.970
and need to be accounted for.

1579
01:11:30.970 --> 01:11:32.150
Even just the fact that

1580
01:11:32.150 --> 01:11:34.950
over the course of a four week period of time

1581
01:11:34.950 --> 01:11:37.190
there are so many studies about,

1582
01:11:37.190 --> 01:11:38.640
if you're in your PMS week,

1583
01:11:38.640 --> 01:11:40.360
bluntly you're probably not

1584
01:11:40.360 --> 01:11:42.400
gonna be performing quite as well

1585
01:11:42.400 --> 01:11:44.310
and that doesn't mean you shouldn't push through,

1586
01:11:44.310 --> 01:11:48.723
and sometimes kind of continued to grind,

1587
01:11:49.600 --> 01:11:51.320
but there should be this acknowledgement that,

1588
01:11:51.320 --> 01:11:54.760
okay, maybe the sort of times we're targeting this week

1589
01:11:54.760 --> 01:11:55.810
can be a little adjusted.

1590
01:11:55.810 --> 01:11:58.950
Or if you're in a program that build for three weeks

1591
01:11:58.950 --> 01:12:00.040
and drops for a week,

1592
01:12:00.040 --> 01:12:03.580
maybe if we try to align that with that week,

1593
01:12:03.580 --> 01:12:04.940
that'd be more beneficial.

1594
01:12:04.940 --> 01:12:09.080
So super kind of sciencey oriented opportunities

1595
01:12:09.080 --> 01:12:12.363
are something that very, very few people talk about.

1596
01:12:13.420 --> 01:12:15.970
But the other thing is that for women,

1597
01:12:15.970 --> 01:12:19.163
how we're built physically and how we develop,

1598
01:12:20.410 --> 01:12:23.950
guys it's just an upward trajectory perpetually-

1599
01:12:23.950 --> 01:12:25.420
Yeah, it's pretty straight forward.

1600
01:12:25.420 --> 01:12:29.040
Girls, there's always that kind of college year slump,

1601
01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:32.320
and that's okay, that's just a thing,

1602
01:12:32.320 --> 01:12:35.410
and maybe one girl doesn't experience that,

1603
01:12:35.410 --> 01:12:37.713
maybe one girl hits it really, really hard,

1604
01:12:38.680 --> 01:12:40.870
but if you're lifted through that

1605
01:12:40.870 --> 01:12:42.820
and you're supported through that,

1606
01:12:42.820 --> 01:12:45.210
then you're gonna get to 22,

1607
01:12:45.210 --> 01:12:48.483
and then you're gonna have that kind of like resurgence of,

1608
01:12:50.370 --> 01:12:52.300
kind of like knowing your body,

1609
01:12:52.300 --> 01:12:57.300
and your body kind of being in this space where it's done-

1610
01:12:57.470 --> 01:13:00.053
Yeah, well, there's a later peak to it, right?

1611
01:13:01.390 --> 01:13:04.370
And understanding that there's gonna be peaks and valleys

1612
01:13:04.370 --> 01:13:05.870
through those interim years,

1613
01:13:05.870 --> 01:13:08.530
and not making a big to-do about that,

1614
01:13:08.530 --> 01:13:10.130
or having it be a referendum

1615
01:13:10.130 --> 01:13:12.060
on how the rest of your career is gonna play out

1616
01:13:12.060 --> 01:13:13.290
I think is really important.

1617
01:13:13.290 --> 01:13:16.760
And I think that change only happens

1618
01:13:16.760 --> 01:13:20.790
when you are staffing these teams with women coaches

1619
01:13:20.790 --> 01:13:22.080
and women psycho,

1620
01:13:22.080 --> 01:13:24.920
it's like you gotta have people who understand this,

1621
01:13:24.920 --> 01:13:28.630
who can help these young women through this process,

1622
01:13:28.630 --> 01:13:32.100
and not hold them to expectations

1623
01:13:32.100 --> 01:13:35.110
that are ultimately not in their long-term best interests

1624
01:13:35.110 --> 01:13:36.190
as an athlete.

1625
01:13:36.190 --> 01:13:39.350
Exactly 'cause I think one thing too is that,

1626
01:13:39.350 --> 01:13:41.850
all of the issues that I've spoken about,

1627
01:13:41.850 --> 01:13:45.330
REDS, Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport,

1628
01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:47.880
which is essentially when you're under fueling,

1629
01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:49.403
and or over-training,

1630
01:13:49.403 --> 01:13:53.810
and, or therefore developing different physical reactions

1631
01:13:53.810 --> 01:13:58.530
such as bone loss, men can experience that as well.

1632
01:13:58.530 --> 01:14:03.260
But the bar at which you start to break down

1633
01:14:03.260 --> 01:14:05.750
is easier for women to hit,

1634
01:14:05.750 --> 01:14:07.510
and it's because physically

1635
01:14:07.510 --> 01:14:09.540
we're supposed to carry a little bit more fat,

1636
01:14:09.540 --> 01:14:11.833
where we have this almost,

1637
01:14:14.250 --> 01:14:16.630
our fail safe of reproductive health

1638
01:14:16.630 --> 01:14:19.620
is gonna drop before a man's does.

1639
01:14:19.620 --> 01:14:21.323
And as a result, we almost,

1640
01:14:22.369 --> 01:14:24.890
it's not that we can't push ourselves as hard,

1641
01:14:24.890 --> 01:14:29.560
but going over the red line too often is more risky.

1642
01:14:29.560 --> 01:14:32.450
There's more opportunities for things to start to break down

1643
01:14:32.450 --> 01:14:34.500
just 'cause biologically we're different.

1644
01:14:35.760 --> 01:14:40.760
And I think that's why having people who understand,

1645
01:14:41.540 --> 01:14:43.450
and can relate, and sympathize,

1646
01:14:43.450 --> 01:14:46.900
and have their own experiences such as other female coaches

1647
01:14:46.900 --> 01:14:50.600
or support staff is always going to be helpful.

1648
01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:53.390
But it's also incredibly important so that

1649
01:14:53.390 --> 01:14:55.820
the men who are also in those positions,

1650
01:14:55.820 --> 01:14:58.890
and also want to be coaching or support staff,

1651
01:14:58.890 --> 01:14:59.800
do the work,

1652
01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:02.823
and put in the time, and educate themselves and,

1653
01:15:03.860 --> 01:15:04.880
I think that's

1654
01:15:04.880 --> 01:15:07.620
can be said about so many different things.

1655
01:15:07.620 --> 01:15:10.840
Just as we need more women in sport,

1656
01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:14.833
we need more diverse individuals getting these positions.

1657
01:15:15.700 --> 01:15:20.700
And I always say that any culture that supports inclusivity,

1658
01:15:20.910 --> 01:15:22.370
and supports diversity,

1659
01:15:22.370 --> 01:15:24.250
and supports this ability to have

1660
01:15:24.250 --> 01:15:25.460
different opinions in a room,

1661
01:15:25.460 --> 01:15:28.540
and different experiences in a room and backgrounds,

1662
01:15:28.540 --> 01:15:31.100
is always going to be a better culture

1663
01:15:31.100 --> 01:15:36.100
than one in which it's just one type of person.

1664
01:15:36.660 --> 01:15:38.730
So for as much as we need more women,

1665
01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:42.090
we also need more people of all sorts of backgrounds

1666
01:15:42.090 --> 01:15:44.930
being able to be in those positions because

1667
01:15:44.930 --> 01:15:48.290
that just the ability to find ways to relate

1668
01:15:49.220 --> 01:15:52.280
with one another, and it's just super powerful.

1669
01:15:52.280 --> 01:15:55.370
Yeah, there's always gonna be the cult of personality

1670
01:15:55.370 --> 01:15:57.730
with certain coaches though.

1671
01:15:57.730 --> 01:16:01.670
It kind of feels like that's the way it is.

1672
01:16:01.670 --> 01:16:03.550
Maybe that can change, I don't know.

1673
01:16:03.550 --> 01:16:05.350
I think when we're talking about this,

1674
01:16:05.350 --> 01:16:07.530
we're talking about biology and physiology,

1675
01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:09.950
but there's also the psychology of all of it, right?

1676
01:16:09.950 --> 01:16:11.220
Absolutely.

1677
01:16:11.220 --> 01:16:15.130
On the one hand, we should point out that

1678
01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:17.280
weight is a factor in performance

1679
01:16:17.280 --> 01:16:20.410
in running and in lots of sports.

1680
01:16:20.410 --> 01:16:23.270
So it's not about, "Hey, we can't talk about that,"

1681
01:16:23.270 --> 01:16:25.247
or, "That's a verboten subject,"

1682
01:16:26.140 --> 01:16:30.460
but maybe don't make your athletes weigh themselves

1683
01:16:30.460 --> 01:16:32.010
in front of the other athletes.

1684
01:16:32.880 --> 01:16:34.260
There's a way of doing this, right?

1685
01:16:34.260 --> 01:16:37.140
So, how do you have a productive,

1686
01:16:37.140 --> 01:16:39.700
if you're a coach or you're an athlete,

1687
01:16:39.700 --> 01:16:43.570
how do you talk about this in a meaningful, productive way?

1688
01:16:43.570 --> 01:16:45.570
Well, I think the first thing is that

1689
01:16:45.570 --> 01:16:49.040
you have to do get yourself educated.

1690
01:16:49.040 --> 01:16:50.940
And whether it's things like SafeSport training,

1691
01:16:50.940 --> 01:16:54.560
or working with a nutritionist yourself or having,

1692
01:16:54.560 --> 01:16:57.080
and also, I'm gonna actually backtrack,

1693
01:16:57.080 --> 01:16:58.370
registered dietician,

1694
01:16:58.370 --> 01:17:00.220
especially if you're with younger athletes,

1695
01:17:00.220 --> 01:17:05.000
I really recommend working with somebody who's done

1696
01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:06.380
the full training

1697
01:17:06.380 --> 01:17:10.580
that's required to have a dietician's degree.

1698
01:17:10.580 --> 01:17:14.210
I think nutritionism is like kind of this buzz word,

1699
01:17:14.210 --> 01:17:17.670
but the truth is the more you're working with true experts

1700
01:17:17.670 --> 01:17:19.580
and people who have devoted their life

1701
01:17:19.580 --> 01:17:21.870
to understanding this,

1702
01:17:21.870 --> 01:17:23.910
the better equipped your going to be

1703
01:17:23.910 --> 01:17:28.190
because most coaches aren't registered dieticians,

1704
01:17:28.190 --> 01:17:30.680
and most coaches maybe have not had

1705
01:17:30.680 --> 01:17:34.180
that level of education on this topic.

1706
01:17:34.180 --> 01:17:36.390
So the more you're leaning on people who have

1707
01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:38.370
is super important.

1708
01:17:38.370 --> 01:17:40.463
And I think another thing is, as a coach,

1709
01:17:41.336 --> 01:17:44.020
you also have to work with somebody

1710
01:17:44.020 --> 01:17:48.210
like a therapist or a psychologist

1711
01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:52.010
to also make sure you're not bringing any of your

1712
01:17:52.010 --> 01:17:54.780
kind of own perspectives onto the track.

1713
01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:57.660
And I think that's really important because

1714
01:17:57.660 --> 01:18:00.440
a lot of these issues we're talking about

1715
01:18:00.440 --> 01:18:03.170
are being talked about really loudly right now.

1716
01:18:03.170 --> 01:18:05.230
But if you were maybe an athlete in the 90s,

1717
01:18:05.230 --> 01:18:09.750
and you yourself kind of went through these experiences

1718
01:18:09.750 --> 01:18:12.130
even if you know they were wrong,

1719
01:18:12.130 --> 01:18:15.540
I think so often people kind of bring their own history,

1720
01:18:15.540 --> 01:18:19.320
and their own maybe discomfort with food into things.

1721
01:18:19.320 --> 01:18:23.378
So I think that kind of first step is take care of yourself,

1722
01:18:23.378 --> 01:18:25.230
get the education that you can,

1723
01:18:25.230 --> 01:18:27.720
work with the professionals that you can,

1724
01:18:27.720 --> 01:18:29.880
and see if you can also kind of find out

1725
01:18:29.880 --> 01:18:33.680
where your own biases lie, that's number one.

1726
01:18:33.680 --> 01:18:36.100
And then number two is really encourage your athletes

1727
01:18:36.100 --> 01:18:38.390
to similarly work with those people.

1728
01:18:38.390 --> 01:18:40.380
And once you've maybe assembled that team,

1729
01:18:40.380 --> 01:18:43.530
or have a list of resources,

1730
01:18:43.530 --> 01:18:46.170
try to find ways to build that into your programming,

1731
01:18:46.170 --> 01:18:50.470
and really make sure that the language around food,

1732
01:18:50.470 --> 01:18:51.720
and the language around weight,

1733
01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:55.253
and body inclusivity is always as positive as you can.

1734
01:18:56.700 --> 01:19:00.020
And if you're not the professional,

1735
01:19:00.020 --> 01:19:01.810
do not pretend to be that.

1736
01:19:01.810 --> 01:19:06.490
So if you are concerned about an athletes weight

1737
01:19:06.490 --> 01:19:08.833
being too heavy, for example,

1738
01:19:11.540 --> 01:19:14.780
that's not really your place to

1739
01:19:16.150 --> 01:19:18.150
give them a weight that they need to hit

1740
01:19:18.150 --> 01:19:20.850
because there is no ideal weight, you don't know,

1741
01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:22.477
the registered dietician would look at you like,

1742
01:19:22.477 --> 01:19:24.040
"That's not a thing.

1743
01:19:24.040 --> 01:19:26.840
You can't give somebody an arbitrary number."

1744
01:19:26.840 --> 01:19:29.420
And so I think the more you kind of

1745
01:19:29.420 --> 01:19:31.570
try to stay in your lane,

1746
01:19:31.570 --> 01:19:34.350
try to direct people to the resources

1747
01:19:34.350 --> 01:19:37.530
that truly are going to help them

1748
01:19:37.530 --> 01:19:39.290
while at the same time educating yourself

1749
01:19:39.290 --> 01:19:40.123
as much as possible is important.

1750
01:19:40.123 --> 01:19:42.272
Right, and if you're a coach

1751
01:19:42.272 --> 01:19:44.960
maybe don't bully or athletes,

1752
01:19:44.960 --> 01:19:46.850
and talk shit about the other athletes

1753
01:19:46.850 --> 01:19:48.000
in front of the other athletes,

1754
01:19:48.000 --> 01:19:49.730
and create some crazy culture of fear

1755
01:19:49.730 --> 01:19:54.470
so that Mary Cain has to go steal cliff bars and

1756
01:19:54.470 --> 01:19:57.240
nibble on them in the dark.
Thank you Nathan.

1757
01:19:57.240 --> 01:19:59.200
You could be caught, right?

1758
01:19:59.200 --> 01:20:01.450
So, look, this whole thing plays,

1759
01:20:01.450 --> 01:20:03.570
I mean, basically in pretty short shrift

1760
01:20:03.570 --> 01:20:07.800
after you end up in Oregon, it's not working out, right?

1761
01:20:07.800 --> 01:20:09.347
How long were you there before you're like,

1762
01:20:09.347 --> 01:20:12.650
"I'm outta here and I'm done with all this."

1763
01:20:12.650 --> 01:20:14.270
So I think the big thing to say is that

1764
01:20:14.270 --> 01:20:18.340
I left Portland by the end of my freshman year.

1765
01:20:18.340 --> 01:20:20.960
So I was out there for about a year.

1766
01:20:20.960 --> 01:20:25.120
But, and this is where I always kick myself looking back,

1767
01:20:25.120 --> 01:20:26.827
that wasn't some big,

1768
01:20:26.827 --> 01:20:29.770
"Oh, this was bad, I'm leaving," moment.

1769
01:20:29.770 --> 01:20:33.782
This was more me apologizing to everybody because-

1770
01:20:33.782 --> 01:20:36.610
You couldn't live up to Alberto's standards.

1771
01:20:36.610 --> 01:20:37.810
Yes.
You couldn't cut it.

1772
01:20:37.810 --> 01:20:38.643
Exactly.

1773
01:20:38.643 --> 01:20:41.637
So I moved home to New York and I told everybody like,

1774
01:20:41.637 --> 01:20:43.690
"I have disordered eating.

1775
01:20:43.690 --> 01:20:46.390
I have been trying to make myself throw up,

1776
01:20:46.390 --> 01:20:49.960
and I can't do this, this is super unhealthy.

1777
01:20:49.960 --> 01:20:51.170
I'm cutting myself,

1778
01:20:51.170 --> 01:20:53.630
my mental health is fully deteriorating.

1779
01:20:53.630 --> 01:20:55.440
I'm really sorry,

1780
01:20:55.440 --> 01:20:58.950
but I just need to figure this out and then I'll come back."

1781
01:20:58.950 --> 01:20:59.810
So that was the plan.

1782
01:20:59.810 --> 01:21:01.340
Just to back up for a second.

1783
01:21:01.340 --> 01:21:03.370
When you, cause you told,

1784
01:21:03.370 --> 01:21:04.760
did you tell Alberto,

1785
01:21:04.760 --> 01:21:07.300
or one of the coaches that you were cutting yourself, right?

1786
01:21:07.300 --> 01:21:09.470
And what was the response?

1787
01:21:09.470 --> 01:21:14.140
It was after this race that had been delayed.

1788
01:21:14.140 --> 01:21:15.240
And so, it was late at night,

1789
01:21:15.240 --> 01:21:20.240
and I was truthfully having a full breakdown.

1790
01:21:20.280 --> 01:21:24.040
And I was really scared that I was gonna hurt myself.

1791
01:21:24.040 --> 01:21:29.040
And so, I went to Alberto and the sports psychs room,

1792
01:21:29.640 --> 01:21:31.457
and I told them like,

1793
01:21:31.457 --> 01:21:33.140
"I just tried to make myself throw up

1794
01:21:33.140 --> 01:21:34.763
multiple times the bathroom.

1795
01:21:35.750 --> 01:21:37.760
I've been cutting myself,"

1796
01:21:37.760 --> 01:21:39.710
at this point the sports had already caught me doing it

1797
01:21:39.710 --> 01:21:41.760
a couple of times, so he knew that I was.

1798
01:21:43.080 --> 01:21:46.730
And I was like, "I don't know what to do.

1799
01:21:46.730 --> 01:21:47.730
I really need help."

1800
01:21:48.910 --> 01:21:53.460
And this is the other thing that always makes me cry.

1801
01:21:53.460 --> 01:21:56.220
I was told that they wanted to go to sleep

1802
01:21:56.220 --> 01:21:57.850
